STATEMENT OF PURPOSE

Applicant: Taha Mahmoodi (Afghan national)
Program: MS, Software Engineering
University: Atılım University
Location: Ankara, Turkey

Dear Admissions Committee,

I am writing to apply to the MS in Software Engineering at Atılım University. Atılım's Software Engineering program — accredited by MÜDEK through 2030, holding the EUR-ACE label, and housed in a Graduate School that established Turkey's first doctoral program in Software Engineering — represents the kind of program that takes software engineering seriously as a discipline, not as an afterthought to computer science. Turkey's welcoming policies toward international students, its cultural and geographic closeness to Afghanistan, and its manageable cost of living relative to Western alternatives make it a country where I can commit fully to graduate study rather than constantly managing logistics.

I started teaching myself to program in the 8th grade, long before it was a formal subject in any classroom I sat in, and that early, self-directed start is still how I approach the field. I made it formal later with a Bachelor of Computer Science from Aria University, graduating in 2025 with a CGPA of 3.68/4.0 (92%) across 144 credit hours. What has held my attention consistently since that first year of self-teaching is not any single course but the systems-level questions — how software is architected, how it fails, and how it is kept running at scale — which is what draws me to Software Engineering and Systems as a specialization.

Alongside my degree, I have spent five years running the technical side of a real company rather than only studying one. As co-founder and CCO of Cyborgtech Creative Agency (2020–2025), I managed the agency's Development division in addition to creative direction — assigning work, reviewing output, and shipping client-facing web applications, mostly remotely and on a task basis that let me keep the company running around my coursework. I have also worked since January 2023 as a Conversational UX QA and Model Tester for Pygmalion.chat, where I test pre-release large language models for security, memory, and response quality; one QA cycle I led identified an 80% improvement in contextual memory reliability between two model versions, directly informing which version shipped to production.

Outside of work and formal study, that same self-taught instinct is what has produced my most substantial technical work. I designed Atlas ERP, an open-source ERP system built to be industry-agnostic, using SAP S/4HANA as the benchmark I measured my own architecture decisions against — a project that taught me more about data modeling and system boundaries than any single course did. I also work on Third Eye Worldwide, a nonprofit delivering assistive technology to blind and low-vision learners across the Global South, where I built a voice-first social platform premised on the blind user being the default user rather than an edge case, plus the CMS and admin dashboard that support it. And when I got tired of losing track of local development servers scattered across my devices, I built LLSGC — a desktop controller with a companion agent that can monitor, restart, and firewall any server bound to a local port.

My specialization is Software Engineering and Systems, and Atılım's program, with its international accreditation and genuine depth in the field, is exactly where I want to study the theoretical and practical foundations of building systems that scale. The program's standing tells me the faculty understands software engineering as a mature discipline.

My longer-term goal is a PhD in software engineering or systems research, and I see this master's as the place where I finally get the formal research grounding that a self-taught, project-driven path has not given me — structured theory, a research group to be accountable to, and faculty who can show me what a real research problem looks like from the inside. I would welcome the opportunity to bring that same self-directed energy into your program.

Sincerely,
Taha Mahmoodi
